1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,325
Welcome to Session four.

2
00:00:02,325 --> 00:00:09,975
This is the session about sequence alignment, and it is where the course stops fetching data and starts drawing conclusions from it.

3
00:00:09,975 --> 00:00:15,275
Session three ended with two related sequences pulled out of the databases.

4
00:00:15,275 --> 00:00:26,625
Today's question is the obvious next one: which residues in the first sequence correspond to which residues in the second, and how much evidence does that correspondence give for a shared ancestor?

5
00:00:26,625 --> 00:00:32,050
That is a harder question than it sounds, and I want to be precise about what we are doing.

6
00:00:32,050 --> 00:00:34,975
An alignment is not an observation.

7
00:00:34,975 --> 00:00:37,300
It is a hypothesis.

8
00:00:37,300 --> 00:00:50,850
It proposes that these residues here descend from the same ancestral position, so that the places where the two sequences differ are substitutions, and the places where one has residues the other lacks are insertions or deletions.

9
00:00:50,850 --> 00:00:56,875
The score we then compute says how plausible that proposal is — under the scoring scheme we chose.

10
00:00:56,875 --> 00:01:00,150
It does not say we have recovered the true history.

11
00:01:00,150 --> 00:01:04,200
That qualification is the thread running through the whole session.

12
00:01:04,200 --> 00:01:14,400
Most of what follows is about the choices that go into the score: how to penalise a gap, which substitution matrix to use, and how to tell a good score from a lucky one.

13
00:01:14,400 --> 00:01:16,800
The session has three blocks.

14
00:01:16,800 --> 00:01:23,925
The first asks why alignment needs an algorithm at all, and builds the dynamic-programming algorithm that answers it.

15
00:01:23,925 --> 00:01:30,575
The second is about scoring: gap penalties, substitution matrices, and whether a score means anything.

16
00:01:30,575 --> 00:01:38,225
The third moves from two sequences to many — multiple alignment, trees, and what a family tells you that a pair cannot.

17
00:01:38,825 --> 00:01:42,050
Here is what we are talking about, concretely.

18
00:01:42,050 --> 00:01:52,500
This is the start of an alignment of human beta-globin against human alpha-globin, scored with BLOSUM sixty-two — two proteins we will keep coming back to all session.

19
00:01:52,500 --> 00:01:54,450
Read the columns.

20
00:01:54,450 --> 00:02:02,325
Where both sequences have a residue, the alignment is claiming those two residues sit at the same position in the common ancestor.

21
00:02:02,325 --> 00:02:06,200
If they are the same residue, we call it an identity.

22
00:02:06,200 --> 00:02:11,900
If they differ, the alignment is claiming a substitution happened on one of the two lineages.

23
00:02:11,900 --> 00:02:18,700
Where one sequence has a residue and the other has a gap, the alignment is claiming an insertion or a deletion.

24
00:02:18,700 --> 00:02:21,525
Every one of those claims could be wrong.

25
00:02:21,525 --> 00:02:27,050
Shift the gaps by one position and you get a different set of claims, and a different score.

26
00:02:27,050 --> 00:02:36,400
That is why the algorithm matters: we want the arrangement that scores highest under our scheme, and we want to find it without being fooled into thinking it is the truth.

27
00:02:37,000 --> 00:02:38,600
First block.

28
00:02:38,600 --> 00:02:45,050
Why does aligning two sequences need a dedicated algorithm, and what does that algorithm look like?

29
00:02:45,650 --> 00:02:53,125
Before the algorithm, the vocabulary, because it gets misused constantly and the misuse causes real confusion.

30
00:02:53,125 --> 00:02:58,850
Two sequences are homologous if and only if they share a common evolutionary origin.

31
00:02:58,850 --> 00:03:01,400
That is the whole definition.

32
00:03:01,400 --> 00:03:04,900
Note what it is not: it is not a percentage.

33
00:03:04,900 --> 00:03:08,900
Homology is a yes-or-no property about ancestry.

34
00:03:08,900 --> 00:03:14,650
You cannot be eighty per cent homologous any more than you can be eighty per cent someone's cousin.

35
00:03:14,650 --> 00:03:16,975
And we never observe it.

36
00:03:16,975 --> 00:03:27,800
The common ancestor is long gone, so homology is always inferred, and the evidence we infer it from is usually the similarity of the sequences — which requires an alignment.

37
00:03:27,800 --> 00:03:36,150
So there is a dependency to keep straight: we align in order to measure similarity, and we use similarity as evidence for homology.

38
00:03:36,150 --> 00:03:38,425
Two measures get used.

39
00:03:38,425 --> 00:03:43,300
Identity is the fraction of aligned positions holding the same residue.

40
00:03:43,300 --> 00:03:51,325
Similarity is looser and also counts residues sharing physicochemical properties — two hydrophobic residues, say.

41
00:03:51,325 --> 00:03:53,925
Both are evidence for homology.

42
00:03:53,925 --> 00:03:55,825
Neither is proof.

43
00:03:55,825 --> 00:04:03,700
Since homology is defined by a common ancestor, homologs come in two kinds, depending on what event separated them.

44
00:04:03,700 --> 00:04:09,825
Orthologs were separated by a speciation: myoglobin in human and myoglobin in mouse.

45
00:04:09,825 --> 00:04:19,675
Paralogs were separated by a gene duplication: alpha-globin and beta-globin, which both descend from a single duplicated ancestral globin gene.

46
00:04:19,675 --> 00:04:22,750
Here is the subtlety that catches people.

47
00:04:22,750 --> 00:04:26,650
Those definitions refer to events, not to genomes.

48
00:04:26,650 --> 00:04:30,225
So paralogs need not sit in the same organism.

49
00:04:30,225 --> 00:04:44,025
Human alpha-globin and mouse beta-globin are paralogs, even though they are in different species, because the event separating them is the alpha-beta duplication — and that duplication happened before human and mouse diverged.

50
00:04:44,025 --> 00:04:49,850
Which means you cannot tell orthologs from paralogs by looking at which species they came from.

51
00:04:49,850 --> 00:04:53,450
You need the order of events, and for that you need a tree.

52
00:04:53,450 --> 00:04:55,975
We build one in the third block.

53
00:04:56,575 --> 00:04:58,825
Now the problem itself.

54
00:04:58,825 --> 00:05:05,875
To measure identity we first have to decide which residues to compare, and that turns out to be the hard part.

55
00:05:05,875 --> 00:05:09,625
If two sequences are the same length, it is trivial.

56
00:05:09,625 --> 00:05:13,700
Put one under the other, walk down the columns, count the matches.

57
00:05:13,700 --> 00:05:14,700
Done.

58
00:05:14,700 --> 00:05:21,750
Real sequences are not the same length, because insertions and deletions accumulate over evolutionary time.

59
00:05:21,750 --> 00:05:29,000
And once lengths differ, where you place one sequence under the other changes the answer — not slightly, but dramatically.

60
00:05:29,000 --> 00:05:31,525
Look at the numbers on this slide.

61
00:05:31,525 --> 00:05:34,700
These are two real globin sequences.

62
00:05:34,700 --> 00:05:39,075
Aligned correctly they are twenty-one and a half per cent identical.

63
00:05:39,075 --> 00:05:46,900
Place them naively — just butt the two N-termini together and start counting — and they look as low as four per cent identical.

64
00:05:46,900 --> 00:05:49,175
Same two sequences.

65
00:05:49,175 --> 00:05:51,800
Same evolutionary history.

66
00:05:51,800 --> 00:05:56,250
A five-fold difference in the answer, purely from where you put them.

67
00:05:56,250 --> 00:05:59,575
So the real problem is not counting matches.

68
00:05:59,575 --> 00:06:01,550
Counting is easy.

69
00:06:01,550 --> 00:06:07,000
The problem is finding the right placement, and that is what the rest of this block is about.

70
00:06:07,600 --> 00:06:13,450
The obvious idea is to try every possible placement and keep whichever scores best.

71
00:06:13,450 --> 00:06:15,800
It is obviously correct.

72
00:06:15,800 --> 00:06:19,850
It is also hopeless, and it is worth seeing how hopeless.

73
00:06:19,850 --> 00:06:27,550
Allowing gaps, the number of ways to align two sequences of length n grows like the binomial coefficient: two n choose n.

74
00:06:27,550 --> 00:06:37,275
For sequences of a hundred residues — which is a small protein, shorter than the globins we have been looking at — that is about nine times ten to the fifty-eighth.

75
00:06:37,275 --> 00:06:39,775
Call it ten to the fifty-ninth.

76
00:06:39,775 --> 00:06:43,050
There is no useful comparison for that number.

77
00:06:43,050 --> 00:06:46,625
It is vastly more than the number of atoms in the Earth.

78
00:06:46,625 --> 00:06:52,500
You cannot enumerate it with any computer that will ever exist, and the sequences are short.

79
00:06:52,500 --> 00:06:55,050
So exhaustive search is out.

80
00:06:55,050 --> 00:07:01,350
But notice we do not actually need to look at most of those alignments, and the reason is the key insight.

81
00:07:01,350 --> 00:07:05,875
Enormous numbers of those candidate alignments share the same beginnings.

82
00:07:05,875 --> 00:07:15,200
If you have already worked out the best way to align the first ten residues against the first eight, every alignment extending that prefix reuses the same answer.

83
00:07:15,200 --> 00:07:20,350
Solve each prefix problem once, store the answer, look it up when you need it again.

84
00:07:20,350 --> 00:07:28,925
That is dynamic programming, and it finds the guaranteed-optimal alignment while never examining the overwhelming majority of the possibilities.

85
00:07:28,925 --> 00:07:38,650
Before we build it, though, there is something simpler worth seeing — because sometimes you do not need an optimal alignment, you just need to know whether there is anything there at all.

86
00:07:39,250 --> 00:07:45,600
A dot plot needs no algorithm whatsoever, and it answers a question the algorithm answers slowly.

87
00:07:45,600 --> 00:07:53,725
The construction: put one sequence along each axis of a grid, and put a dot wherever the row residue and the column residue match.

88
00:07:53,725 --> 00:07:55,400
That is it.

89
00:07:55,400 --> 00:08:03,150
A run of consecutive matches then lines up as a diagonal line, and the human eye picks a diagonal out of noise instantly.

90
00:08:03,150 --> 00:08:05,350
Compare the two panels.

91
00:08:05,350 --> 00:08:08,925
On the left, every identical pair gets a dot.

92
00:08:08,925 --> 00:08:19,850
With twenty amino acids, any two residues match about one time in twenty by chance, so the plot is mostly noise — you can just about convince yourself there is something there.

93
00:08:19,850 --> 00:08:26,100
On the right, a dot is drawn only where a window of ten residues contains at least five identities.

94
00:08:26,100 --> 00:08:30,075
The noise disappears and the diagonal is unmistakable.

95
00:08:30,075 --> 00:08:36,375
What a dot plot gives you is qualitative: is there a relationship here, and roughly whereabouts is it?

96
00:08:36,375 --> 00:08:39,775
What it does not give you is a score or an alignment.

97
00:08:39,775 --> 00:08:43,025
It will not tell you which residue pairs with which.

98
00:08:43,025 --> 00:08:46,600
It is not a historical curiosity, by the way.

99
00:08:46,600 --> 00:08:54,300
BLAST's web output still shows you one, because a picture answers "is there anything here" faster than reading a number does.

100
00:08:54,900 --> 00:09:05,375
Three patterns are worth learning to recognise, and these three panels are built to show them cleanly — each piece is twenty-five residues of beta-globin, assembled to order.

101
00:09:05,375 --> 00:09:11,475
The first is a sequence against itself: A plus B plus C, compared with A plus B plus C.

102
00:09:11,475 --> 00:09:15,100
One long unbroken diagonal, corner to corner.

103
00:09:15,100 --> 00:09:18,825
That is what a single end-to-end alignment looks like.

104
00:09:18,825 --> 00:09:22,450
The second is A plus B plus A against itself.

105
00:09:22,450 --> 00:09:31,725
Now there are parallel diagonals off the main one, and they are there because the A piece appears twice: the first copy matches the second copy as well as itself.

106
00:09:31,725 --> 00:09:34,575
Parallel diagonals mean a repeat.

107
00:09:34,575 --> 00:09:39,000
The third is A plus B plus C against A plus reversed-B plus C.

108
00:09:39,000 --> 00:09:46,825
The outer pieces still give diagonals running the usual way, but the middle one runs the other way — perpendicular to the rest.

109
00:09:46,825 --> 00:09:51,100
A diagonal with the opposite slope means an inverted segment.

110
00:09:51,100 --> 00:09:54,875
Learn these three and you can read most real dot plots.

111
00:09:54,875 --> 00:10:00,750
But for a score, and for an actual residue-by-residue alignment, we need dynamic programming.

112
00:10:01,350 --> 00:10:03,525
So let us build it.

113
00:10:03,525 --> 00:10:11,375
Dynamic programming makes a matrix, called V, with one extra row and one extra column to handle the starts of the sequences.

114
00:10:11,375 --> 00:10:15,450
One sequence runs down the side, the other along the top.

115
00:10:15,450 --> 00:10:18,975
The meaning of each cell is the thing to hold on to.

116
00:10:18,975 --> 00:10:28,300
Cell V at i, j holds the score of the best possible alignment of the first i residues of one sequence against the first j residues of the other.

117
00:10:28,900 --> 00:10:31,450
Not an alignment — the best one.

118
00:10:31,450 --> 00:10:40,025
If that is true for every cell, then the bottom-right cell holds the score of the best alignment of both sequences entire, which is what we want.

119
00:10:40,025 --> 00:10:43,700
Now, how do we fill a cell so that the claim stays true?

120
00:10:43,700 --> 00:10:48,125
Think about what the last column of that best alignment can possibly be.

121
00:10:48,125 --> 00:10:50,425
There are only three options.

122
00:10:51,025 --> 00:11:00,625
Either the two residues are aligned to each other, or the residue from the side sequence is aligned to a gap, or the residue from the top sequence is aligned to a gap.

123
00:11:00,625 --> 00:11:02,875
There is no fourth case.

124
00:11:03,475 --> 00:11:07,025
Each option points at a cell we have already solved.

125
00:11:07,025 --> 00:11:19,175
Align the two residues, and the rest of the alignment is the best alignment of the two shorter prefixes — the cell diagonally up and left — plus the substitution score for this pair, a match or a mismatch.

126
00:11:19,175 --> 00:11:24,775
Put a gap against the side residue and you come from the cell above, plus a gap penalty.

127
00:11:25,375 --> 00:11:31,175
Put a gap against the top residue and you come from the cell to the left, plus a gap penalty.

128
00:11:31,175 --> 00:11:36,350
So: compute all three candidates, keep the largest, and remember which one you kept.

129
00:11:36,350 --> 00:11:41,200
That last part matters — the stored choice is what we follow in the traceback.

130
00:11:41,200 --> 00:11:48,825
The slide shows this for cell V at one, one, with a gap penalty of one: three candidates computed, the biggest retained.

131
00:11:48,825 --> 00:11:53,050
Every other cell in the matrix is filled by exactly this rule.

132
00:11:53,050 --> 00:11:57,600
Nothing changes except the numbers going into the three candidates.

133
00:11:58,200 --> 00:12:00,100
Now watch it run.

134
00:12:00,100 --> 00:12:10,875
The order matters only in that each cell's three neighbours — above, left, and diagonally up-left — must already be done, so filling row by row from the top, left to right, works fine.

135
00:12:10,875 --> 00:12:15,875
Let me do a small one completely, because it is worth seeing every number once.

136
00:12:15,875 --> 00:12:24,075
Align G-C-A-T down the side against G-A-C along the top, with match plus one, mismatch minus one, and gap minus one.

137
00:12:24,675 --> 00:12:30,175
The first row and first column are the base cases: aligning something against nothing.

138
00:12:30,175 --> 00:12:40,925
There is only one way to do that, all gaps, so the entries are zero, minus one, minus two, minus three across, and zero, minus one, minus two, minus three, minus four down.

139
00:12:40,925 --> 00:12:44,350
Take the top-left interior cell, G against G.

140
00:12:44,350 --> 00:12:47,575
Diagonal: zero plus a match, plus one.

141
00:12:47,575 --> 00:12:51,000
From above: minus one plus a gap, minus two.

142
00:12:51,600 --> 00:12:54,225
From the left: the same, minus two.

143
00:12:54,225 --> 00:12:58,075
The largest is plus one, and it came from the diagonal.

144
00:12:58,075 --> 00:13:08,900
Now C against G: the diagonal gives minus one plus a mismatch, minus two; from above, plus one minus one is zero; from the left, minus two minus one is minus three.

145
00:13:08,900 --> 00:13:11,300
Zero wins, from above.

146
00:13:11,300 --> 00:13:14,350
Carry on like that and the matrix fills.

147
00:13:14,950 --> 00:13:21,700
The bottom-right corner comes out at zero, which is the score of the best global alignment of these two sequences.

148
00:13:21,700 --> 00:13:28,325
The same procedure works at real scale, with a real substitution matrix instead of plus and minus one.

149
00:13:28,925 --> 00:13:35,150
The figure shows the completed matrix for two protein sequences of twelve and thirteen residues.

150
00:13:35,150 --> 00:13:39,175
It is the same rule applied a hundred and fifty-six times.

151
00:13:39,175 --> 00:13:43,950
Nothing about the algorithm changed; only the size of the picture did.

152
00:13:44,550 --> 00:13:47,175
The matrix gives us the score.

153
00:13:47,175 --> 00:13:52,625
It does not yet give us the alignment — for that we walk backwards through the choices we stored.

154
00:13:53,225 --> 00:14:00,400
Start at the bottom-right corner, because that is the cell holding the score of the best alignment of both sequences in full.

155
00:14:00,400 --> 00:14:07,525
Then ask, at each step, which of the three candidates this cell's value actually came from, and step to that neighbour.

156
00:14:07,525 --> 00:14:12,900
Each step writes one column of the alignment, and which column depends on the direction.

157
00:14:13,500 --> 00:14:18,875
A diagonal step means the two residues were aligned to each other — write them as a pair.

158
00:14:18,875 --> 00:14:23,050
A step upwards means the side residue was aligned to a gap.

159
00:14:23,050 --> 00:14:27,225
A step to the left means the top residue was aligned to a gap.

160
00:14:27,225 --> 00:14:30,675
Keep stepping until you reach the top-left corner.

161
00:14:30,675 --> 00:14:36,500
Because you are walking backwards, the alignment comes out backwards too, so reverse it at the end.

162
00:14:37,100 --> 00:14:44,475
That is a detail, but it is the kind of detail that costs an hour when you implement this yourself, which you will do in the lab.

163
00:14:44,475 --> 00:14:54,150
On our small example, the path in bold runs from the bottom-right back to the top-left and reads out as G-C-A-T on top against G-gap-A-C underneath.

164
00:14:54,150 --> 00:14:58,025
Checking it against the score: G matches G, plus one.

165
00:14:58,025 --> 00:15:00,875
C is aligned to a gap, minus one.

166
00:15:00,875 --> 00:15:03,300
A matches A, plus one.

167
00:15:03,900 --> 00:15:07,100
T is mismatched against C, minus one.

168
00:15:07,100 --> 00:15:11,125
Total: zero — which is exactly what the corner cell said.

169
00:15:11,125 --> 00:15:13,725
One more thing worth noticing.

170
00:15:13,725 --> 00:15:21,500
If two candidates tie, there are two equally optimal alignments, and the algorithm has no principled way to prefer either.

171
00:15:21,500 --> 00:15:24,600
Implementations pick by convention.

172
00:15:24,600 --> 00:15:32,175
So "the optimal alignment" is sometimes "an optimal alignment", and a tool that shows you one answer may be hiding a tie.

173
00:15:32,775 --> 00:15:42,050
The algorithm we have just built is Needleman-Wunsch, and it produces a global alignment: both sequences end to end, in their entirety, gaps and all.

174
00:15:42,050 --> 00:15:48,575
The traceback starts in the bottom-right corner precisely because that corner means "all of both sequences".

175
00:15:48,575 --> 00:15:51,100
Often that is not what we want.

176
00:15:51,100 --> 00:15:55,525
We may want only the best-matching sub-region — a local alignment.

177
00:15:55,525 --> 00:16:00,675
Smith-Waterman does that, and the remarkable thing is how little has to change.

178
00:16:00,675 --> 00:16:03,125
Three changes, all small.

179
00:16:03,125 --> 00:16:07,450
First, any negative value in the matrix is reset to zero.

180
00:16:07,450 --> 00:16:14,100
Second, the traceback starts not at the bottom-right corner but at the highest-scoring cell anywhere in the matrix.

181
00:16:14,100 --> 00:16:19,150
Third, it stops as soon as it reaches a zero, rather than running to the corner.

182
00:16:19,150 --> 00:16:22,625
Think about what flooring the matrix at zero does.

183
00:16:22,625 --> 00:16:27,975
A cell going negative means the alignment up to that point is worse than nothing.

184
00:16:27,975 --> 00:16:33,100
Resetting it to zero says: forget everything before here and start fresh.

185
00:16:33,100 --> 00:16:39,225
The alignment is free to begin anywhere and end anywhere, which is exactly what "local" means.

186
00:16:39,225 --> 00:16:44,100
One change to the recurrence turns a global algorithm into a local one.

187
00:16:44,700 --> 00:16:53,425
Which of the two you use is a biological decision, not a technical one, and getting it wrong produces nonsense that still looks like an alignment.

188
00:16:53,425 --> 00:16:58,525
Use global when you expect the two sequences to correspond along most of their length.

189
00:16:58,525 --> 00:17:06,900
Two myoglobins from different species are the obvious case: same protein, same architecture, same length to within a few residues.

190
00:17:06,900 --> 00:17:12,075
Forcing them end to end is right, because end to end is the real relationship.

191
00:17:12,075 --> 00:17:15,350
Use local when they may share only a part.

192
00:17:15,350 --> 00:17:19,750
One domain shared between two large multidomain proteins.

193
00:17:19,750 --> 00:17:22,950
A short motif buried in a long sequence.

194
00:17:22,950 --> 00:17:26,175
A fragment against a full-length protein.

195
00:17:26,175 --> 00:17:30,425
Here is what happens if you choose wrong in the dangerous direction.

196
00:17:30,425 --> 00:17:39,200
Force a global alignment onto two sequences that share only a domain, and the algorithm must account for every residue outside the shared region.

197
00:17:39,200 --> 00:17:50,600
It does so with a long tail of meaningless gaps, which cost points, and the genuine shared domain gets buried in the middle of that mess — sometimes scoring worse than an unrelated pair would.

198
00:17:50,600 --> 00:17:54,300
The relationship is real and the alignment hides it.

199
00:17:54,300 --> 00:17:58,550
So: how much of the two sequences do you expect to correspond?

200
00:17:58,550 --> 00:18:01,750
Answer that first, then pick the algorithm.

201
00:18:01,750 --> 00:18:07,125
And since the answer depends heavily on how gaps are charged, gaps are where we go next.

